Block C — Name Lookup and Function Behavior
C1. Function Hiding — Name Lookup Before Signature Matching
      — derived class function hides ALL base class functions with the same name
      — why this is NOT the same as overriding
      — how the compiler's name lookup works
      — the surprising consequence: overloads in the base are hidden too

C2. using Declarations — Restoring Hidden Names
      — bringing base class overloads back into scope
      — syntax and placement
      — when this is needed

C3. Function Overriding — Same Signature in Derived Class
      — what override means precisely
      — how it differs from hiding
      — what gets called depends on how the function is called
Block D — Static vs Dynamic Binding
D1. Static Binding — Compile-Time Dispatch
      — how the compiler decides which function to call at compile time
      — calling through a base pointer or reference
      — the wrong-function problem

D2. The Problem Static Binding Creates
      — concrete example showing wrong function being called
      — why this makes polymorphism impossible without virtual

D3. virtual Keyword — Dynamic Dispatch
      — what virtual does
      — how it changes which function is called
      — the runtime mechanism

D4. vtable and vptr Internals — Machine Level
      — what the vtable is
      — where the vptr lives inside the object
      — how dispatch works at the assembly level
      — memory overhead of virtual functions

D5. override and final (C++11)
      — what override enforces and why it was added
      — what final does to a class and to a function
      — the bugs override prevents

D6. Pure Virtual Functions and Abstract Classes
      — syntax: = 0
      — what abstract class means
      — what cannot be done with abstract class objects
      — interface design pattern in C++

D7. Virtual Destructors — Why Mandatory
      — what happens when delete is called through a base pointer
        without a virtual destructor
      — memory leak and undefined behavior
      — the rule: if any function is virtual, destructor must be virtual
Block E — Slicing and Runtime Type
E1. Object Slicing
      — what happens when a derived object is passed by value to a base parameter
      — why the derived portion is lost
      — how to prevent it (always use pointer or reference for polymorphic types)

E2. dynamic_cast and RTTI
      — what RTTI is
      — safe downcasting through dynamic_cast
      — what happens on failure (nullptr for pointer, exception for reference)
      — when dynamic_cast is appropriate vs when it signals a design problem
      — typeid operator
Block F — Multiple Inheritance
F1. Multiple Inheritance — Basic Syntax
      — a class deriving from two or more base classes
      — memory layout with multiple bases
      — constructor order with multiple bases

F2. Ambiguity in Multiple Inheritance
      — same member name in two base classes
      — how to resolve with explicit qualification

F3. The Diamond Problem
      — concrete example showing the problem
      — two copies of the grandparent in memory
      — which copy gets used — and the resulting bugs

F4. Virtual Inheritance — Solving the Diamond
      — virtual base class syntax
      — one shared copy of the grandparent
      — memory layout with virtual base
      — constructor order with virtual inheritance
      — the most-derived class must initialize the virtual base directly
Block G — Advanced Patterns
G1. Covariant Return Types
      — a virtual function in a derived class can return a more derived type
      — when this is useful

G2. Constructor Inheritance — using Base::Base (C++11)
      — inheriting constructors from the base class
      — what is inherited and what is not
      — when this is useful vs when it causes problems

G3. CRTP — Curiously Recurring Template Pattern
      — what it is
      — static polymorphism without vtable overhead
      — how it differs from virtual dispatch
      — real use cases
